research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-8-{"id":"def-associates-in-z","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisibility_(ring_theory)"],"rationale":"Wikipedia: \"Elements a and b of an integral domain are associates if both a | b and b | a\". The item is that definition specialised to Z and phrased as an explicit binary relation on Z x Z; it deliberately claims nothing beyond the definition. Proof component not-applicable (definition, no Proof section).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-9-{"id":"lem-associates-characterisation","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisibility_(ring_theory)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_(ring_theory)"],"rationale":"Wikipedia (ring theory) gives the equivalence of mutual divisibility with b = ua for a unit u in an integral domain; the Divisor page gives the Z form \"if a divides b and b divides a, then a = b or a = -b\"; the unit page fixes the units of Z as {1,-1}, which turns that into |a| = |b|. The item merges the three into one TFAE and adds the equivalence-relation and class-{a,-a} clauses. Proof is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-10-{"id":"lem-int-bounded-above-has-greatest","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://sites.millersville.edu/bikenaga/number-theory/greatest-integer-function/greatest-integer-function.html"],"rationale":"Millersville number-theory notes state it exactly: \"Suppose S is a nonempty set of integers which is bounded above: There is an integer M such that x < M for all x in S. Then S has a largest element.\" Convention delta: the source bounds strictly (x < M), the item non-strictly (s <= b); the two are interchangeable by passing to M+1. The item adds the dual statement for a lower bound and the uniqueness clause. The proof follows the sourced route (reflect S through the bound into N and apply well-ordering).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-11-{"id":"def-common-divisor-and-gcd","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Wikipedia: gcd is \"the greatest positive integer d such that d is a divisor of both a and b\" and \"gcd(0, 0) is commonly defined as 0. This preserves the usual identities for GCD\". The item defines the common-divisor set D(a,b), takes the greatest element where it exists, adopts the same gcd(0,0) = 0 convention, and argues for it in place. Convention delta: the item defines gcd for all integer pairs (not only positive ones), nonnegative by construction. Proof component not-applicable; the inline existence argument (nonempty and bounded above, so the greatest-element lemma applies) is locally authored and flagged to Alpha.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-12-{"id":"lem-gcd-basic-values","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Wikipedia Properties give gcd(a,0) = |a|, commutativity, sign invariance and gcd(a,1) = 1. The item collects these plus gcd(a,a) = |a| and the \"gcd >= 1 unless a = b = 0\" clause, each explicitly checked at the zero boundary. Proof is a locally written direct argument from the definition.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:13:{"id":"thm-bezout-identity","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A9zout%27s_identity","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Wikipedia Bezout: \"Let a and b be integers with greatest common divisor d. Then there exist integers x and y such that ax + by = d\", and d is \"the smallest positive integer\" of that form; the GCD page states the same alternative definition \"where a and b are not both zero\". The item states both clauses with the not-both-zero hypothesis explicit, which is the delta from the bare Wikipedia theorem box. The proof is the standard least-positive-element argument, adapted to run well-ordering on the preimage in N.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-14-{"id":"cor-common-divisor-divides-gcd","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Wikipedia: \"Every common divisor of a and b is a divisor of gcd(a, b)\" and \"the common divisors of a and b are exactly the divisors of their GCD\". The item adds the four-condition characterisation (d >= 0, d | a, d | b, every common divisor divides d) and asserts it at (0,0) as well, which the source does not spell out. Proof is locally written, splitting on the (0,0) case.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-15-{"id":"lem-gcd-euclidean-step","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm"],"rationale":"Wikipedia states the invariance as \"gcd(x,y) = gcd(y, x - zy)\". The item strengthens it to equality of the whole sets of common divisors and drops any inequality on r, so that it applies to any decomposition a = qb + r. Proof is the standard two-inclusion argument via linearity, with the added (0,0)-branch bookkeeping.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-16-{"id":"thm-euclidean-algorithm","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm"],"rationale":"Wikipedia: the descent \"r_{k-2} = q_k r_{k-1} + r_k, with r_{k-1} > r_k >= 0\" and \"the last non-zero remainder r_{N-1} being equal to gcd(a,b)\". The item formalises the descent as a function supplied by the recursion theorem, restricts to b > 0 to match the published division algorithm, and proves termination from the remainder range. Proof follows the sourced argument in this library formalism.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-17-{"id":"cor-extended-euclidean-bezout-coefficients","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extended_Euclidean_algorithm"],"rationale":"Wikipedia gives the coefficient recurrences s_{i+1} = s_{i-1} - q_i s_i, t_{i+1} = t_{i-1} - q_i t_i alongside the remainders, the invariant \"r_i = a s_i + b t_i\", and the conclusion \"gcd(a,b) = r_k = a s_k + b t_k\". The item packages the same recursion as a single map on Z^6 driven by the recursion theorem. Proof follows the sourced invariant by induction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-18-{"id":"def-coprime","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime_integers"],"rationale":"Wikipedia: a and b are coprime when \"the only positive integer that is a divisor of both of them is 1\", equivalently gcd = 1; and \"1 and -1 ... are the only integers that are coprime with 0\". The item takes gcd(a,b) = 1 as the definition and records the three degenerate pairs, each read off the basic-values lemma. Proof component not-applicable.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
