research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"lem-gcd-scaling","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Wikipedia states the distributive property only for a positive multiplier: \"If m is a positive integer, then gcd(m a, m b) = m gcd(a, b)\". The item extends it to every integer c with |c| on the right and asserts it at c = 0 and at (a,b) = (0,0); that extension is the delta and is what makes the gcd(0,0) convention forced. Proof is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"cor-gcd-quotients-coprime","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Wikipedia Properties state it exactly: \"If gcd(a, b) = d, then gcd(a/d, b/d) = 1\". The item adds the explicit well-definedness of a/d and b/d by cancellation in Z, and the hypothesis d != 0. Proof is the standard scaling-identity argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"def-lcm","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Least_common_multiple"],"rationale":"Wikipedia: lcm is \"the smallest positive integer that is divisible by both a and b\", and \"some authors define lcm(a, 0) as 0 for all a, since 0 is the only common multiple of a and 0\". The item adopts exactly that pair of clauses and argues that the value 0 is forced rather than chosen. Proof component not-applicable; the inline existence argument is locally authored and flagged to Alpha.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"thm-gcd-lcm-product","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Least_common_multiple","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Both clauses are sourced: \"for any other common multiple n of a and b, m divides n\" and \"gcd(a,b) * lcm(a,b) = |a b|\" (also on the GCD page). The item states them for every integer pair including the zero cases. Proof is the standard route through a/d, b/d coprime.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"lem-subgroups-of-z-are-cyclic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclic_group"],"rationale":"Wikipedia: \"All subgroups of Z are of the form <m> = mZ, with m a positive integer\", and \"All subgroups and quotient groups of cyclic groups are cyclic\". The item corrects and strengthens this: the generator ranges over the natural numbers (so the trivial subgroup, which the Wikipedia phrasing omits, is included at n = 0) and it is unique. Its own Remarks state the delta from the weaker published example. Proof is the standard least-positive-element plus division argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:26:{"id":"thm-gcd-generates-the-subgroup","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A9zout%27s_identity","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Least_common_multiple"],"rationale":"The first identity is Bezout in subgroup form: Wikipedia states \"the integers of the form az + bt are exactly the multiples of d\". The second is the sourced ideal characterisation on the LCM page: \"the least common multiple of a and b can be characterised as a generator of the intersection of the ideals generated by a and b\". The item states both for (Z,+) with the canonical nonnegative generator. Proof is locally assembled from Bezout, divisibility and the subgroup classification.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"ex-euclidean-algorithm-worked","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm"],"rationale":"The construction is Wikipedia own worked example, line for line: \"1071 = 2 x 462 + 147\", \"462 = 3 x 147 + 21\", \"147 = 7 x 21 + 0\", \"the algorithm ends with 21 as the greatest common divisor of 1071 and 462\". The item adds the back-substitution 21 = 7*462 - 3*1071, which is the standard reverse reading of the same three divisions. Verification follows the sourced computation and re-checks each product locally.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:28:{"id":"ex-bezout-coefficients-not-unique","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A9zout%27s_identity","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diophantine_equation"],"rationale":"The solution family is sourced: Wikipedia Bezout gives the other solutions as (x0 - k b/d, y0 + k a/d), which is the item family (x0 + t b/d, y0 - t a/d) with t = -k; the Diophantine page states the same parametrisation. The numerical instance is computed from the sourced 1071/462 example, and the second pair (19,-44) is a local instantiation at t = 1, checked in step 6.1. Proof follows the sourced derivation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-29-{"id":"ex-gcd-with-zero","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"Both values are sourced: \"gcd(a, 0) = |a|\" and \"gcd(0, 0) is commonly defined as 0. This preserves the usual identities for GCD\". The item turns that parenthetical justification into an explicit derivation: the scaling identity at c = 0, and at (a,b) = (0,0) with c = 1+1, each forces the value 0. The derivation is locally written; nothing in it is a new mathematical claim.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-30-{"id":"ex-linear-diophantine-equation-solvability","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diophantine_equation"],"rationale":"Wikipedia states the criterion exactly: \"This Diophantine equation has a solution ... if and only if c is a multiple of the greatest common divisor of a and b\". The witnesses 6x + 15y = 9 (solvable) and 6x + 15y = 7 (not) are a local choice of small instances, each verified in steps 3.1 and 4.1. Proof follows the sourced Bezout-scaling argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl:31:{"id":"ex-gcd-and-lcm-as-subgroups-of-z","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A9zout%27s_identity","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Least_common_multiple"],"rationale":"The content is an instantiation of two sourced statements (Bezout in the form \"the integers of the form az + bt are exactly the multiples of d\", and lcm as a generator of the intersection of the ideals) at the pair (12,18). No source exhibiting that particular pair was located; the pair is a routine local choice, not an invented claim, and every value in it is verified in steps 1.1, 2.1 and 3.1. Verification is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-32-{"id":"ex-euclidean-algorithm-on-consecutive-fibonacci-numbers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm","https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanAlgorithm.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence"],"rationale":"Two established results, neither located in the exact form the item states. (1) Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are coprime: standard, and a special case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_gcd(m,n); no clean HTTP-200 statement surfaced (ProofWiki returns 403 to the fetcher). (2) The division count: Wikipedia and MathWorld both record that consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the worst case for the Euclidean algorithm (Lame 1844), and the standard form of that analysis is that (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) needs exactly n divisions, which is the item claim with n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a final 2) is the routine content of the same analysis. Both are recoverable restatements of established mathematics, not invented claims, so the tag is ai-altered under evidence established-knowledge and REQUIRES Alpha concurrence. The indexing convention F_0 = 0 is stated by the item and matches the Wikipedia zeroth-element convention. Proof is a locally written double induction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-33-{"id":"cex-divides-a-product-without-dividing-a-factor","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclid%27s_lemma","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime_integers"],"rationale":"The refuted claim is the prime-free reading of Euclid lemma, whose failure is exactly what the sourced coprimality hypothesis exists to prevent: \"If an integer n divides the product ab of two integers, and is coprime with a, then n divides b\". The witness (6,4,9) is a local choice of small integers; no source exhibiting it was located, but it is a routine instance rather than an invented claim, and every clause is verified in steps 1.1 to 1.5. Refutation is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-34-{"id":"cex-common-divisors-of-zero-have-no-greatest-element","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divisor"],"rationale":"The mathematical content is the sourced justification of the gcd(0,0) convention: every integer divides 0 (Divisor page: \"Every integer divides zero\"), so the common divisors of (0,0) are all of Z and Wikipedia defines gcd(0,0) = 0 by convention rather than as a maximum. The item states that as an explicit refutation of \"the maximum always exists\" and observes the greatest-element lemma is not contradicted because Z is not bounded above. Refutation is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-35-{"id":"fs-gcd-times-lcm-equals-the-product","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Least_common_multiple","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"],"rationale":"The false claim is the unsigned identity, which the Wikipedia LCM page itself writes as \"gcd(a,b) * lcm(a,b) = ab\" while the correct general form on both pages is |a b|; so the item records a real and sourced convention trap rather than a manufactured falsehood. The refuting witness (-2,3) is a local choice of a small pair with ab < 0, verified in steps 1.1 to 3.1, and the Remarks note every such pair refutes it. Refutation is locally written.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-02"}
research/audit/wave0-number-theory.provenance.jsonl-36-{"id":"ex-euclidean-algorithm-on-consecutive-fibonacci-numbers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_algorithm","https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclideanAlgorithm.html","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibonacci_sequence"],"rationale":"Two established results, neither located in the exact form the item states. (1) Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are coprime: standard, and a special case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_gcd(m,n); no clean HTTP-200 statement surfaced (ProofWiki returns 403 to the fetcher). (2) The division count: Wikipedia and MathWorld both record that consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the worst case for the Euclidean algorithm (Lame 1844), and the standard form of that analysis is that (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) needs exactly n divisions, which is the item claim with n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a final 2) is the routine content of the same analysis. Both are recoverable restatements of established mathematics, not invented claims, so the tag is ai-altered under evidence established-knowledge and REQUIRES Alpha concurrence. The indexing convention F_0 = 0 is stated by the item and matches the Wikipedia zeroth-element convention. Proof is a locally written double induction.\n\nALPHA CONCURS (2026-08-02). Both components are standard established mathematics. (1) gcd(F_{k+1}, F_k) = 1 for all k is a textbook result and the k-th case of gcd(F_m,F_n) = F_{gcd(m,n)}. (2) The division count is the standard statement of the Euclidean algorithm's Fibonacci worst case (Lame 1844): on (F_{n+2}, F_{n+1}) with n >= 1 the algorithm performs exactly n divisions, which is the item's claim at n = m+1; the quotient pattern (1 repeated, then a terminal 2 at (F_3,F_2) = (2,1)) is the routine content of the same analysis, not an invented embellishment. Alpha verified the claim numerically at m = 0,1,2: (2,1) -> 1 division q=2; (3,2) -> 2 divisions q=1,2; (5,3) -> 3 divisions q=1,1,2. The F_0 = 0 indexing is stated by the item and matches the standard convention, and the item explicitly disclaims Lame's optimality statement. ai-altered under the established-knowledge waiver; not ai-generated, since nothing here is AI-invented.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-02","alpha_reviewer":"audit-alpha (claude-opus-5)"}
